// Hadamard: creating a superposition.
//
// Concept
//   H maps |0> to an equal superposition of |0> and |1>. The qubit is not
//   secretly in one of the two states — it is in both, with equal amplitude,
//   and that distinction is what `interference.qf` makes observable.
//
// Expected quantum behaviour
//   Amplitudes 1/sqrt(2) on each of |0> and |1>.
//
// Expected measurement results
//   0 and 1 each about 50% of shots. Any individual shot is one or the other.
//
// Mathematical interpretation
//   H = (1/sqrt 2) [[1, 1], [1, -1]], so H|0> = (|0> + |1>)/sqrt(2).
//   The Born rule gives |1/sqrt(2)|^2 = 1/2 for each outcome.
//
//   Note the amplitude is 0.7071, not 0.5. Amplitudes are square roots of
//   probabilities, and they can be negative or complex — which probabilities
//   cannot.
//
// @expect qubits=1 clbits=1 gates=1
// @expect-state |0>=0.5
// @expect-state |1>=0.5
// @expect-support 0,1
// @expect-frequency 0=0.5
// @expect-frequency 1=0.5

qubit q;
bit result;

h q;

measure q -> result;
